keywords:
  - large language models
  - LLM agents
  - agent scaffolding
  - long-horizon agents
  - Vending-Bench
  - ablation study
  - reproducibility
  - external memory
abstract: >-
  A seed-matched component-ablation study testing which part of a scaffolding
  harness lets a weak 4-billion-parameter language model (gemma3:4b) succeed on a
  200-day business-operations simulator with Vending-Bench economics. Across
  N=100 matched seeds, a naive one-call-per-day model baseline fails 100% of
  runs, a deterministic scripted policy 59%, and the full harness 46%. Removing
  persistent structured state collapses performance to 100% failure (below the
  naive baseline), while removing the rarely-binding financial gates does not
  measurably change median performance or failure rate. A seat-level ablation
  identifies model-mediated pricing as a necessary contributor to the harness's
  advantage. Includes the paper, the Kotlin/JVM harness, and stdlib
  reproducibility scripts; the raw run databases ship as a release asset.
